HB.add({
  id: "05",
  intro: `Chunking splits documents into pieces small enough to embed, match precisely and fit in the prompt — but big enough to keep their meaning. Chunk size and boundaries change retrieval quality more than most model choices, and enrichment (headers, summaries, questions) decides whether a chunk can be found by the words users actually type. This section covers basic and advanced strategies and how to tune them with evaluation.`,
  groups: [
    { id: "5.1", title: "Basic Strategies", items: [
      { t: `Chunk Size`,
        s: `How big each piece of a document is — usually measured in tokens, typically 200 to 500.`,
        h: `Every chunk becomes one vector and one unit of retrieval. Small chunks (100–200 tokens) match questions precisely because each covers one idea, but they lose surrounding context and more of them are needed per answer. Large chunks (800+ tokens) keep context, but their single vector is an "average" of several topics, so they match each specific question weakly, and fewer fit in the prompt. The best size depends on your documents (FAQ answers are short, legal clauses medium, narratives long), your questions, and the embedding model's limit.`,
        u: `Decide chunk size for every pipeline and, ideally, per document type: short chunks for FAQs and product specs, medium for policies and manuals, larger (or parent–child) for narrative reports and transcripts. Re-evaluate when you change the embedding model, because models differ in how well they represent longer text. Always measure the choice with recall@k on your golden questions rather than copying a number from a tutorial, and record the chosen size as part of the pipeline version.`,
        p: `The wrong chunk size causes two opposite failures. Too small: the answer is split across chunks, each fragment lacks the context to rank well, and the model receives half the information ("carry over up to 5 days" without "they expire on 31 March"). Too big: one chunk mixes several topics, its vector matches nothing strongly, it pushes other evidence out of the context budget, and the LLM has to find one sentence in a wall of text. Tuning size is one of the cheapest, highest-impact improvements in RAG.`,
        e: `Illustrative: a team tests 200, 400 and 800-token chunks on the same 100 HR questions. 200-token chunks find many exact sentences but often miss the condition in the next paragraph; 800-token chunks rank poorly for specific questions; 400 tokens with structure-aware boundaries gives the best recall@5. They record 400 as the setting for policy documents and keep 200 for the FAQ collection, where each answer is short.` },
      { t: `Chunk Overlap`,
        s: `Repeating a little text from the end of one chunk at the start of the next, so nothing important is cut in half.`,
        h: `With fixed-size or sliding-window splitting, each chunk starts some tokens before the previous one ended — commonly 10–20% of the chunk size (e.g. 50 tokens for 400-token chunks). A sentence that sits on a boundary then appears complete in at least one chunk. Overlap is less necessary when you split at natural boundaries (headings, paragraphs), because those rarely cut sentences. Too much overlap creates near-duplicate chunks that waste index space and fill the top results with copies.`,
        u: `Use overlap with fixed-size, token-window and sliding-window chunking, and for long paragraphs that must be split inside a section. Keep it small with structure-aware chunking (or zero when chunks end at paragraph boundaries). Deduplicate overlapping hits when building the context, so the LLM does not read the same sentences twice, and account for overlap when estimating index size. Typical starting points are 10–15% for policies and manuals and up to 20% for transcripts.`,
        p: `Without overlap, a rule and its condition can land in different chunks: chunk 7 ends with "Unused days carry over up to 5 days" and chunk 8 starts with "only if approved by the manager". A question about carry-over then retrieves chunk 7 alone and the answer is wrong. Overlap makes such boundary splits much less likely at a small storage cost. Overlap is not free, though: it increases the number of chunks, embedding time and index size, and it can make duplicate passages crowd the top results if deduplication is missing.`,
        e: `400-token chunks with 50 tokens of overlap: the sentence "Unused days carry over up to 5 days, only if approved by the manager" sits across a boundary, but because of the overlap it appears whole at the start of the next chunk. Both chunks may be retrieved; context assembly keeps the one containing the full sentence and drops the duplicate. Measured on the golden set, adding 50 tokens of overlap to fixed-size chunks raised recall slightly, while doubling it to 100 added storage without further gain (illustrative).` },
      { t: `Fixed-size Chunking`,
        s: `Cutting text every N tokens (or characters), regardless of meaning.`,
        h: `The text is tokenised and cut into windows of N tokens, each starting N − overlap tokens after the previous one. It is simple, fast, predictable and works on any text. It ignores sentences, paragraphs, headings and tables, so it often cuts mid-sentence and mixes the end of one topic with the start of another. Character-based versions are even cruder, because characters do not match model limits. Its main appeal is predictability: chunk counts, index size and embedding cost are easy to estimate in advance.`,
        u: `Use it as a quick baseline, for unstructured text dumps (logs, transcripts without punctuation), and when you need predictable chunk counts for capacity planning. For business documents, prefer structure-aware or recursive splitting and use fixed-size only as a fallback inside very long paragraphs. It is also a useful control in experiments: if a smarter method does not beat fixed-size chunks on your golden set, it is not worth its complexity. Keep the size in tokens, not characters, so chunks respect model limits.`,
        p: `It gives a working pipeline in minutes, which is useful for proving that RAG fits a use case and for measuring how much smarter chunking helps. Its weaknesses — broken sentences, mixed topics, tables cut apart — show up quickly on real questions and motivate better strategies. Teams that stay with it in production usually see recurring failures on tables, lists and conditions that sit just across a boundary. Measuring those failures is the best argument for moving on.`,
        e: `A transcript with no paragraph breaks is cut into 300-token windows with 50 tokens of overlap. It works acceptably for "what did we decide about the budget?". The same method applied to a policy PDF cut a table in half and produced the wrong allowance value, which led the team to structure-aware chunking for policies. The comparison on the golden set made the decision easy: structure-aware chunks answered noticeably more table and policy questions correctly (illustrative). The transcript collection kept fixed-size windows because it had no structure to use.`,
        c: `
def fixed_chunks(tokens, size=400, overlap=50):
    step = size - overlap
    return [tokens[i:i + size] for i in range(0, max(len(tokens) - overlap, 1), step)]` },
      { t: `Token-based Sizing`,
        s: `Measuring chunk size with the model's own tokenizer instead of characters or words.`,
        h: `Embedding models and LLMs have limits in tokens, and the same text can produce very different token counts depending on language, numbers, code and the tokenizer itself. Token-based sizing counts tokens with the tokenizer of the embedding model (for chunks) or the LLM (for prompts), and splits so each chunk stays under the target. Splitters such as LangChain's can be constructed from a Hugging Face tokenizer for exactly this purpose.`,
        u: `Always use it for chunking, especially for multilingual corpora, code, tables full of numbers and documents with long identifiers. Keep a safety margin under the embedding model's limit for the query/passage prefix and the contextual header that are added before embedding. Recount chunk sizes whenever you switch embedding models, because a new tokenizer can turn previously safe chunks into truncated ones. Store the token count on each chunk for monitoring and budgets.`,
        p: `A "1,000-character" chunk can be 250 tokens of English but 700 tokens of another script or of dense code. If it exceeds the embedder's 512-token limit, the end is silently truncated and never influences the vector — the key fact at the end of the chunk becomes unsearchable. Token-based sizing prevents this invisible loss. It also keeps prompt budgets honest: if chunk token counts are stored, context assembly can add chunks until the LLM budget is reached without guessing.`,
        e: `This platform counts chunk length with the multilingual-e5 tokenizer and caps chunks at 400 tokens, leaving room for "passage: " and the "Title &gt; Section" header within e5's 512-token limit. A log of truncation events stays at zero. Each chunk's token count is stored in the database, so context building can sum them to stay within the 1,200-token source budget. When a new embedding model with a different tokenizer was tested, the stored counts showed which chunks would have been truncated.`,
        c: `
from transformers import AutoTokenizer
tok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained("intfloat/multilingual-e5-small")
def n_tokens(text):
    return len(tok.encode(text, add_special_tokens=False))` },
      { t: `Recursive Character Splitting`,
        s: `Split by the largest natural separator first (paragraphs), and only if a piece is still too big, split it by smaller ones (lines, sentences, words).`,
        h: `The splitter tries separators in order — for example "\\n\\n" (paragraphs), "\\n" (lines), ". " (sentences), " " (words). If a piece fits the size limit, it stays whole; if not, it is split with the next separator, recursively. Small neighbouring pieces are then merged back up to the size limit, with optional overlap. LangChain's RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter is the best-known implementation, and it can measure size in tokens. Separators can be customised per format: code splitters use class and function boundaries, Markdown splitters use headings.`,
        u: `Use it as a strong general-purpose default for text without reliable headings: articles, emails, wiki pages, transcripts with punctuation. Combine it with heading-based splitting for structured documents (split by headings first, then recursively inside long sections). Measure sizes in tokens (construct the splitter from your tokenizer) and add a small overlap. For documents with headings, run a heading-based split first and recursive splitting only inside sections that are too long.`,
        p: `It avoids most mid-sentence cuts without needing a document structure, so it is far better than fixed-size splitting at almost no extra cost. It still does not know about tables or topics, which is why structure-aware and semantic chunking exist for harder documents. Because it relies only on whitespace and punctuation, it can still cut a table or list in an awkward place, and it cannot tell when the topic changes inside a long paragraph.`,
        e: `A long blog post is split at paragraph breaks; one 900-token paragraph is too big, so it is split further at sentence boundaries into two ~450-token pieces. No sentence is cut, and each chunk reads naturally. On a wiki export with few headings, switching from fixed-size to recursive splitting removed nearly all mid-sentence chunk starts, which made citations look clean and reranker scores more stable.`,
        c: `
from langchain_text_splitters import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter
splitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter.from_huggingface_tokenizer(
    tok, chunk_size=400, chunk_overlap=50, separators=["\\n\\n", "\\n", ". ", " "])
chunks = splitter.split_text(document_text)` },
      { t: `Sentence-based Chunking`,
        s: `Build chunks from whole sentences, adding sentences until the size limit is reached.`,
        h: `The text is split into sentences with a sentence segmenter (spaCy, NLTK, or rules for simple cases; multilingual segmenters for other languages). Consecutive sentences are grouped until the next one would exceed the token limit; then a new chunk starts, optionally repeating the last sentence as overlap. A sentence is never cut. Sentence segmentation needs care with abbreviations ('e.g.', 'Dr.'), numbers and bullet lists, which simple rules mis-split.`,
        u: `Use it for prose: articles, reports, transcripts, emails and policies without clear headings. It is also the basis of sentence-window retrieval, where each sentence is indexed separately and expanded at query time. Use a segmenter that supports your languages, and combine it with paragraph boundaries so chunks also respect the author's structure. It pairs well with small overlap of one sentence.`,
        p: `Half-sentences confuse embedding models and the LLM, and they make citations look broken. Sentence-based chunks are always readable and quotable, which improves both retrieval and the user's trust in cited passages. It also gives better material to rerankers, which score complete sentences more reliably than fragments. The remaining weakness is topic mixing: a chunk can still contain sentences about two topics if they are adjacent.`,
        e: `A news article becomes chunks of 5–8 complete sentences each. When a question matches the third chunk, the cited passage reads as a clean, complete excerpt rather than starting mid-sentence. For an interview transcript, sentence-based chunks of about 300 tokens made answers quote complete statements from the speaker, which users found much easier to trust than fragments that started mid-sentence. The reranker also scored these complete-sentence chunks more consistently.` },
      { t: `Paragraph-based Chunking`,
        s: `Use each paragraph — or a few small ones together — as a chunk.`,
        h: `Paragraph breaks (blank lines, or paragraph elements from the parser) usually mark one idea. Each paragraph becomes a chunk; very small paragraphs are merged with neighbours up to a minimum size, and very large ones are split by sentences. The section heading above is attached as context. It works best when the parser preserves paragraph breaks correctly, which is another reason parsing quality matters.`,
        u: `Well-written documents with clear paragraphs: policies, guides, FAQs, documentation and books. It works well together with section paths, because each paragraph is a natural unit to cite. Combine it with a minimum size (merge very short paragraphs) and a maximum size (split very long ones by sentences). Keep the section heading as the chunk's header. It is also easy to explain to content owners, who can predict how their documents will be split.`,
        p: `It follows the author's own topic boundaries, which are usually better than any automatic guess. Chunks stay coherent and easy to cite, and answers rarely need text from the middle of another paragraph. Badly written documents with giant paragraphs or one-sentence paragraphs need the size limits; otherwise chunks become either too big or too fragmented.`,
        e: `Each FAQ answer paragraph becomes one chunk with its question as the header ("Q: Can I work from home? A: …"). Retrieval returns exactly the answer paragraph, and the citation shows the question it belongs to. Merging one-line paragraphs with their neighbours kept the FAQ chunks between 80 and 300 tokens, and each still contained exactly one question and its answer. Content owners could predict exactly how their FAQ would be chunked, which made reviews easy.` },
      { t: `Sliding Window Chunking`,
        s: `A fixed-size window moves through the text by a step smaller than the window, so chunks overlap heavily.`,
        h: `With window W and stride S (S &lt; W), every chunk shares W − S tokens with the next one; for example W = 300 and S = 150 gives 50% overlap. Every passage appears in at least two chunks, so a relevant passage is almost never cut badly. The cost is about W ÷ S times more chunks (more storage, more embedding work) and many near-duplicate results that must be deduplicated. It is equivalent to fixed-size chunking with a large overlap, so the same token-based sizing rules apply.`,
        u: `Use it for dense unstructured text where answers often sit at arbitrary positions — contracts without headings, long transcripts, legal text — and for small corpora where storage is not a concern. Deduplicate and merge overlapping hits before building the context. For large corpora, prefer structure-aware chunking with modest overlap; reserve heavy sliding windows for the specific documents where boundary splits keep causing failures. Measure the storage impact before applying it widely. It is also a practical fix when parsing cannot recover structure from old scanned documents.`,
        p: `It strongly reduces "answer split across chunks" failures in text without structure. The trade-off is index size and duplicate results, which is acceptable for high-value, small collections but wasteful for large ones. Duplicate results are the main operational cost: without merging overlapping hits, the context may contain the same clause three times and the citations become confusing. It is a blunt tool, though: it fixes boundary problems by brute force rather than by understanding the text, so it should not replace structure-aware chunking where structure exists.`,
        e: `A 30-page supply contract without headings is windowed at 300 tokens with a 150-token stride. Every clause appears fully in at least one window, so "what is the penalty for late delivery?" retrieves a window that contains the whole penalty clause. Context assembly merges overlapping windows from the same contract into one continuous passage, so the LLM reads the penalty clause once with a single citation. The index for that contract was about twice the size of a non-overlapping version, which was acceptable for a single high-value document.`,
        n: `Deduplicate overlapping hits before sending them to the LLM, or the context fills with near-identical text.` },
      { t: `Structure-aware (Header) Chunking`,
        s: `Split at the document's own structure — headings and sections — never crossing a section boundary.`,
        h: `Using the parsed structure tree (or Markdown headings), each section or subsection becomes a chunk; long sections are split by paragraphs or sentences inside the section; tables stay whole (or are split by rows with the header repeated); and every chunk keeps its section path, such as "Leave policy &gt; Annual leave". Chunks never contain the end of one section and the start of the next. It depends on good heading detection during parsing, so parsing and chunking quality are linked.`,
        u: `Make it the default for business documents: policies, manuals, contracts, standards, specifications, CVs, wikis and runbooks — anything with headings. Combine it with contextual headers (title + section path) for embedding and reranking. Cap section chunks at a token limit and split long sections inside themselves, keeping the section path on every piece. Merge very short sections with their neighbours or attach them to their parent heading.`,
        p: `It keeps one topic per chunk and gives every chunk a meaningful label. Questions about sick leave never retrieve annual-leave text that happened to share a fixed-size window, and citations can name the exact section. It also makes citations meaningful and evaluation easier: you can label relevance by section, and those labels stay valid even when chunk sizes change inside sections. Most production RAG systems for business documents settle on this approach after comparing alternatives.`,
        e: `"## Annual leave" and "## Sick leave" always become separate chunks, so "do I need a doctor's note?" retrieves only the sick-leave chunk. This platform chunks by structure with a 400-token cap and keeps tables whole. Citations in answers show the section path, for example 'Leave policy &gt; Sick leave', which helps users verify the right rule at a glance.`,
        c: `
from langchain_text_splitters import MarkdownHeaderTextSplitter
splitter = MarkdownHeaderTextSplitter([("#", "h1"), ("##", "h2"), ("###", "h3")])
for doc in splitter.split_text(markdown):
    print(doc.metadata, doc.page_content[:60])   # {'h1': 'Leave policy', 'h2': 'Annual leave'} ...` },
      { t: `Chunk Size vs Model Limit`,
        s: `A chunk must fit the embedding model's maximum input — often 512 tokens — including any prefix and header added before embedding.`,
        h: `Text beyond the model's maximum sequence length is silently truncated by the tokenizer. Budget: model limit − role prefix ("passage: ") − contextual header ("Title &gt; Section") − a safety margin. Rerankers have similar limits for question + passage together, so very long chunks are also truncated at reranking time. Some libraries warn about truncation, many do not, so check it yourself.`,
        u: `Check it whenever you pick a chunk size or switch embedding models; long-context embedders (8k tokens) allow larger chunks, small ones (512) do not. Log a warning whenever a chunk is truncated. Also check the reranker's limit, since question plus chunk must fit there too. When chunks must be large, use a long-context embedder or parent–child retrieval instead of exceeding the limit.`,
        p: `Truncation is invisible: the chunk is stored and displayed in full, but its vector only represents the beginning. The key sentence at the end is unsearchable, and nobody notices because nothing errors. It also biases retrieval towards the start of long chunks, so information placed at the end of sections (often exceptions and conditions) is systematically harder to find. Checking token counts once at ingestion removes this risk completely.`,
        e: `e5 models read 512 tokens. Chunks of at most 400 tokens leave about 100 tokens for "passage: " and a header like "Balamurugan Thangadurai CV &gt; PROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCE", so nothing is cut off during embedding. A quick audit script counted tokens for every chunk and found none above 512, confirming that no text was being lost silently. The same audit runs automatically after every pipeline change.` },
    ]},
    { id: "5.2", title: "Advanced Strategies", items: [
      { t: `Semantic Chunking`,
        s: `Cut where the meaning changes: embed each sentence and split where consecutive sentences stop being similar.`,
        h: `Split the text into sentences and embed each one. Compute the similarity between neighbouring sentences (or small windows of sentences). Where similarity drops sharply — below a threshold or in the lowest percentile — a new chunk starts. The result is chunks that each cover one topic, of varying lengths. Minimum and maximum sizes are enforced so chunks are neither tiny nor huge. A percentile-based threshold adapts to each document, whereas a fixed similarity value may split too much or too little.`,
        u: `Use it for long text without headings: meeting transcripts, interviews, essays, narrative reports and chat logs. For structured documents, headings are usually a better signal and much cheaper. Use it selectively: run it on document types without headings and keep heading-based chunking elsewhere. Evaluate it against recursive splitting on your own transcripts before adopting it. It also suits chat logs, where topic shifts happen without any formatting.`,
        p: `Fixed-size and recursive splitting can put two topics in one chunk when there is no structural break, which weakens the chunk's vector for both topics. Semantic chunking finds topic boundaries even when the author did not mark them. Its cost and unpredictability are the trade-off: chunk sizes vary, ingestion takes longer, and results depend on the embedding model used for boundary detection. It is most valuable exactly where structure is missing, so use it there rather than everywhere.`,
        e: `A one-hour meeting transcript is split automatically where the discussion moves from budget to hiring to the office move. The question "what did we decide about hiring?" retrieves the hiring chunk only, instead of a window that is half budget discussion. Measured on 40 meeting questions, semantic chunks improved recall over fixed windows, while adding about a minute of embedding time per hour of transcript (illustrative).`,
        c: `
import numpy as np
def semantic_chunks(sentences, embed, threshold=0.75):
    vecs = embed(sentences)                       # normalized vectors
    chunks, current = [], [sentences[0]]
    for i in range(1, len(sentences)):
        if float(np.dot(vecs[i - 1], vecs[i])) < threshold:
            chunks.append(" ".join(current)); current = []
        current.append(sentences[i])
    return chunks + [" ".join(current)]`,
        n: `It costs one embedding per sentence at ingestion and can produce uneven chunk sizes — enforce limits.` },
      { t: `Agentic / LLM-based Chunking`,
        s: `Asking an LLM to decide where to split a document, or to group related passages into titled chunks.`,
        h: `The LLM reads the document (or a window of it) and returns chunk boundaries, often with a title and summary for each chunk. Some approaches let the model assign each paragraph to an existing chunk or start a new one. It can follow meaning, references and topic shifts that rules miss, but it is slow, costly at scale and not perfectly reproducible. The output is validated so every part of the document lands in exactly one chunk and nothing is lost.`,
        u: `High-value, small corpora with poor formatting: scanned regulations, messy contracts, old manuals converted from images, or documents where chunk quality matters more than ingestion cost. Cache the results so re-processing does not repeat the LLM calls. Store the LLM's chunk titles as section paths, which also improves citations. Run it in batch at ingestion, never at question time, and keep a rule-based fallback for when the LLM fails.`,
        p: `Some documents have no reliable structure, so rule-based splitting produces mixed or fragmented chunks. An LLM can impose sensible structure on them. The trade-off is cost and variability, which is why it is usually reserved for the documents that need it. Results also vary between runs and model versions, so pin the model and prompt and treat changes as pipeline releases that require evaluation. Use it where its quality gain is measurable; elsewhere, rule-based chunking is cheaper and reproducible.`,
        e: `An LLM groups the clauses of a scanned regulation into "Scope", "Obligations", "Reporting" and "Penalties" chunks, each with a title. Questions about penalties now retrieve one coherent chunk instead of fragments spread across pages. The titles produced by the LLM also became section paths in citations, so answers cite 'Regulation 12 &gt; Penalties' instead of a page number only. Re-running it with the same pinned model and prompt reproduces the same chunks.` },
      { t: `Proposition Chunking`,
        s: `Breaking text into atomic facts — propositions — each a short, self-contained sentence.`,
        h: `An LLM rewrites each passage into minimal standalone statements, resolving pronouns and adding the subject: "She joined in 2021 and leads four engineers" becomes "Asha joined Acme in 2021." and "Asha leads four engineers." Each proposition is embedded separately (often linked to its source passage), so retrieval can match single facts very precisely. Propositions are usually short — one fact per sentence — so they produce many more vectors than normal chunks.`,
        u: `Fact-heavy content where questions target single facts: CVs, product specifications, regulations, encyclopaedic content, knowledge bases of short facts. Link every proposition to its source passage and send the passage, not just the proposition, to the LLM, so answers have enough context. Use it selectively for fact-dense collections. Avoid it for narrative documents, where meaning depends on flow rather than single facts.`,
        p: `Long chunks match single-fact questions weakly, and pronouns lose their meaning when text is split ("she", "it", "the company"). Propositions make each fact findable on its own. The cost is an LLM call per passage at ingestion and many more vectors in the index. Errors in the LLM rewrite (wrong subject, lost condition) become errors in retrieval, so a sample of propositions should be reviewed against the original text.`,
        e: `"She joined in 2021 and leads four engineers" (from Asha's CV) becomes two propositions naming Asha. The question "how many engineers does Asha lead?" matches the second proposition almost exactly, and the answer cites the CV paragraph it came from. Index size grew about five times for the CV collection, which was acceptable because the collection is small and fact lookups became noticeably more precise. Each proposition links back to its source paragraph, so citations still open the original text.` },
      { t: `Parent-Child (Small-to-Big)`,
        s: `Search with small chunks for precision, but give the LLM their larger parent section for context.`,
        h: `Each section (the parent, e.g. 1,500 tokens) is split into small children (e.g. 200 tokens). Only children are embedded and searched; each stores its parent's ID. At query time, the best children are found, mapped to their parents, deduplicated (several children may share a parent) and the parents are sent to the LLM within the token budget. Children can also be sentences or propositions, with parents as sections or whole short documents.`,
        u: `Use it when matching needs precision but answers need surrounding detail: policies with conditions spread across paragraphs, technical manuals, contracts, research reports. It is built into LlamaIndex and LangChain (ParentDocumentRetriever). Limit the number of parents per answer (for example three to four) so context stays within budget, and choose parent size to fit the LLM's window. Store parents in the database or a document store keyed by ID.`,
        p: `Small chunks match well but lack context; large chunks give context but match poorly. Parent–child retrieval decouples the unit you search from the unit you read, getting both benefits. The cost is a larger context per answer, so the number of parents must be limited. It also reduces duplicate context: several children from the same section collapse into one parent instead of several overlapping fragments. It is one of the most widely used advanced patterns because it improves answers without changing models.`,
        e: `A 150-token child "carry over up to 5 days" matches the question best; the LLM receives its parent, the whole "Annual leave" section, which also contains "they expire on 31 March" and "not during probation", so the answer is complete. Without parent expansion, the answer would have missed the probation exception, which sat two paragraphs away from the best-matching child chunk. The extra context added about 600 tokens to the prompt, well within budget.`,
        c: `
children = vector_search(question, top_k=10)
parent_ids = list(dict.fromkeys(c.payload["parent_id"] for c in children))  # keep order, dedupe
context = [parent_store[pid] for pid in parent_ids[:4]]` },
      { t: `Hierarchical Chunking`,
        s: `Chunking at several levels at once — document, section, paragraph — and retrieving at the level each question needs.`,
        h: `Chunks are created for each level of the structure tree, with links between levels. Broad questions match section- or document-level chunks (often summaries); specific questions match paragraph-level chunks. Retrieval can search all levels, or start at the top and drill down. Auto-merging retrieval climbs up a level when many children of one parent are retrieved. Each level can use its own embedding text, for example section summaries for upper levels and raw text for paragraphs.`,
        u: `Long structured documents — manuals, books, regulations, technical standards — where users ask both overview and detail questions. Use summaries for the upper levels so broad questions match well, and keep the original text at the leaves for citations. Route questions by type to the right level, or search all levels and let reranking decide. It suits documentation portals where users ask both overview and detail questions.`,
        p: `One chunk size cannot serve both "summarise chapter 3" and "what is the fee in clause 3.2.1?". Hierarchical chunking gives each kind of question a matching unit of text. It also helps long documents fit small context windows: an overview answer can use a few section summaries instead of dozens of detailed chunks. Without it, broad questions return a scattered set of small chunks that never adds up to a good overview, while detail questions get diluted large chunks.`,
        e: `"Summarise the leave policy" retrieves the section-level chunk (or its summary); "how many sick days are paid?" retrieves the paragraph about sick-day pay. Both come from the same document, at different levels. Both answers cite the same document, but at different levels: the overview cites the section, the specific answer cites the paragraph and page. Measured on the golden set, broad questions improved most, while detail questions stayed as good as before (illustrative).` },
      { t: `Sentence Window Retrieval`,
        s: `Index single sentences for precise matching, then return each matched sentence with a few sentences around it.`,
        h: `Each sentence is embedded on its own, storing the IDs (or text) of its neighbours. After retrieval, each hit is replaced by a window — for example the sentence plus three before and three after — and overlapping windows are merged. Window size is a setting to tune: two sentences may be enough for FAQs, five for legal text. Because single sentences are short, their embeddings are precise but miss context, which the window restores.`,
        u: `Precise fact lookup in dense prose: legal text, scientific papers, long reports. Available as a node post-processor in LlamaIndex. It suits corpora where single sentences carry decisive facts, such as terms and conditions, research findings and regulations. Merge overlapping windows from the same document before building the context, and tune the window size on your golden set.`,
        p: `The single best-matching sentence often lacks the context needed to answer or to interpret it correctly ("this does not apply to contractors" — what does not?). The window brings that context back without diluting the match. It also gives very precise citations: the matched sentence can be highlighted while the window gives the reader enough context. The trade-off is a large index (one vector per sentence) and some extra work to merge windows, which is acceptable for precision-critical collections.`,
        e: `The sentence "Payment is due within 30 days" matches; the window adds the next sentence, "Late payments incur a 2% monthly fee", so the answer about payment terms is complete. In the interface, the matched sentence is highlighted inside its window, so users see both the exact evidence and its context. The same index also answers very specific questions such as 'what is the late fee?' with a single precise sentence.` },
      { t: `Late Chunking`,
        s: `Embed the whole document first with a long-context model, then cut the token vectors into chunks and pool each part.`,
        h: `A long-context embedding model processes the full document (or a long section) in one pass, so every token's vector reflects the whole document's context. The token vectors are then split at chunk boundaries and averaged per chunk. Each chunk vector "knows" what "it" or "the company" refers to, without adding any text to the chunk. The document must fit the model's context, so very long documents are processed in long windows rather than all at once.`,
        u: `Documents full of references to earlier content (reports, contracts, articles), when you use a long-context embedding model that supports it (for example models with 8k-token windows). It requires an embedding model with a long context window and access to token-level outputs, so check model support before planning around it. Compare it with contextual headers, which are simpler and often enough. It is most useful for reports, articles and contracts where pronouns and references are common.`,
        p: `Normal chunk embeddings forget context from outside the chunk, so a chunk saying "its revenue grew 20%" does not match "Acme revenue growth". Late chunking keeps document context in each vector at no extra token cost. It adds no text to chunks and no LLM calls, which makes it cheaper than LLM-written context, but it depends on specific model capabilities. It is a newer technique, so evaluate it against simpler options on your own data before adopting it in production.`,
        e: `In a company report, a chunk saying "its revenue grew 20% in Q3" still matches the question "Acme revenue growth in Q3", because its token vectors were computed while the model could see "Acme" earlier in the document. Contextual headers alone would only add the document title; late chunking also captures references made earlier in the text, such as which product 'it' refers to. Recall on reference-heavy questions improved in testing, while simple lookups stayed the same (illustrative).` },
      { t: `Code-aware (AST) Chunking`,
        s: `Splitting source code by its syntax tree — functions, classes, methods — instead of by lines.`,
        h: `The code is parsed with the language's AST module or a universal parser such as tree-sitter. Each function or class becomes a chunk with its file path, signature and docstring as a header; very large classes are split per method, keeping the class name in the header. Imports and module docstrings can form their own chunk. Comments and docstrings stay with their code, because they explain intent in natural language.`,
        u: `Code search, coding assistants, internal SDK and API documentation, and answering "where is X implemented?" questions across repositories. Index each code chunk twice if possible: the code itself for exact identifier search and a natural-language description for semantic search. Keep file paths and repository names as metadata for filters and citations. Use the same approach for configuration files and infrastructure code, split by resources or blocks.`,
        p: `Line-based chunks cut functions in half and lose which class a method belongs to, so code retrieval returns fragments that cannot be understood or reused. AST chunks are complete, meaningful units. It also respects language syntax, so chunks can be parsed, linted or even executed, which is impossible with arbitrary line ranges. Function-level chunks also map naturally to code owners and tests, which helps assistants explain who maintains a piece of code and how it is tested.`,
        e: `<code>InvoiceService.calculate_tax()</code> becomes one chunk with the header "billing/service.py &gt; InvoiceService &gt; calculate_tax". The question "how is tax calculated on invoices?" retrieves the whole method with its docstring. A follow-up question 'which function calls calculate_tax?' can be answered by searching chunks that mention the identifier, because each function is its own unit. Engineers now get whole methods as answers, ready to read or adapt, instead of fragments that start halfway through a loop.`,
        c: `
import ast
def python_chunks(source, path):
    tree = ast.parse(source)
    for node in ast.walk(tree):
        if isinstance(node, (ast.FunctionDef, ast.ClassDef)):
            yield {"header": f"{path} > {node.name}",
                   "code": ast.get_source_segment(source, node)}` },
      { t: `Table-aware Chunking`,
        s: `Keeping tables whole when they fit, and otherwise splitting them by rows with the header repeated in every piece.`,
        h: `The chunker recognises table elements from parsing. A small table becomes one chunk together with its caption and the sentence that introduces it. A large table is split into groups of rows, each starting with the caption and header row. Optionally, a short summary of the whole table is indexed as well, for broad questions. The caption and units travel with every piece, so each slice is self-explanatory.`,
        u: `Any document with tables: price lists, allowance tables, specifications, schedules, financial statements and comparison tables. Decide per table whether row slices, row sentences or both suit the questions users ask. Keep units and footnotes with every slice, because they change how the numbers must be read. Small tables should stay whole, with their surrounding sentence, because splitting them helps nothing.`,
        p: `A table split by a generic chunker produces rows without headers ("B | 4000 | 2500"), which mean nothing to search or to the LLM, and numbers end up paired with the wrong labels. Table-aware chunking keeps every number interpretable. It also prevents a subtle ranking problem: header-less slices of numbers rarely match any question, so without headers whole tables effectively disappear from search.`,
        e: `A 120-row allowance table becomes six chunks of 20 rows, each starting with "Travel allowance by grade and city (2026)" and "| Grade | Mumbai | Pune |". Any row's value can be read correctly from its chunk. A table summary chunk ('Daily allowance per city and grade, 2026') also answers 'which cities have travel allowance rates?' directly. The allowance questions that previously returned wrong values were answered correctly after re-chunking.` },
      { t: `Multimodal Chunking`,
        s: `Chunks that keep images, charts or slides together with the text that explains them.`,
        h: `A figure is grouped with its caption and the paragraph that references it ("see Figure 3"); the image's caption from a vision model is stored as text; slides keep title, bullets, notes and images together. Alternatively, whole page images are embedded with a multimodal model (ColPali) and shown to a vision LLM at answer time. The image itself is stored with a link from the chunk, so it can be shown or passed to a vision model at answer time.`,
        u: `Manuals with diagrams, slide decks, scientific papers, product catalogues and scanned forms. Show the image next to the answer when its chunk is cited, so users can see what was described. Skip decorative images to save cost. It is most useful in technical manuals and training material, where instructions refer to figures constantly.`,
        p: `Images separated from their explanation are unfindable and unexplainable; text separated from its figure loses the visual evidence. Keeping them together makes both searchable and citable. It also enables answers that need visual evidence: a vision-capable LLM can look at the retrieved diagram itself, not just its caption. It also avoids a common citation problem: citing a paragraph that says 'as shown below' without showing what is below.`,
        e: `"Figure 3: Wiring diagram", the vision model's caption of the diagram, and the paragraph "connect the wires as shown in Figure 3" form one chunk. The question "how do I wire the ZX-200?" retrieves all three together. The answer shows the diagram thumbnail next to the cited text, and users confirmed that seeing the image made the instructions much clearer. Support tickets about wiring dropped after the assistant started showing the relevant diagram with each answer.` },
    ]},
    { id: "5.3", title: "Chunk Enrichment & Tuning", items: [
      { t: `Contextual Chunk Headers`,
        s: `Putting "Document title &gt; Section" at the top of each chunk before embedding and reranking, so the chunk says what it is about.`,
        h: `A header is built from metadata — document title (often from the file name), section path, and sometimes date or entity — and prepended to the text that is embedded and scored by the reranker. The stored chunk text stays unchanged for display and citation. The cost is a few extra tokens per chunk at ingestion and reranking; no LLM call is needed. The header can also include the document type or date when those help distinguish similar documents.`,
        u: `Use it almost always — it is free and very effective, especially for documents whose sections never repeat the subject (CVs, product manuals, policies with short sections). Also index the title and section as boosted BM25 fields for the keyword side. Keep headers short (one line) so they do not dominate the chunk's vector. Regenerate them when titles or section names change, which happens automatically if headers are built at ingestion.`,
        p: `Many chunks never name their subject: a CV's experience section never says the person's name; a policy paragraph says "employees receive…" without saying which policy. Such chunks cannot match questions that name the subject, and rerankers score them as unrelated. Headers fix this at almost no cost. They also improve rerankers, which only see the chunk text: with the header, the reranker knows which document and section a passage belongs to.`,
        e: `In this platform, the header "Balamurugan Thangadurai CV &gt; PROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCE" raised the reranker score of the experience chunk for "balamurugan experience" from −9.6 (discarded) to +7.0 (top result). Together with the other v2 changes, answers went from containing 3% to 93% of the expected facts. The same headers also help BM25, because file names and section paths are indexed as boosted fields, so typo-tolerant keyword search also finds the right section.`,
        c: `
def with_header(text, filename, section_path):
    title = filename.rsplit(".", 1)[0].replace("_", " ")
    return f"{title} > {section_path}\\n{text}" if section_path else f"{title}\\n{text}"
vector = embed("passage: " + with_header(chunk.text, doc.filename, chunk.section_path))` },
      { t: `Contextual Retrieval (LLM-generated context)`,
        s: `An LLM writes one or two sentences explaining where each chunk fits in its document; that context is added before embedding and keyword indexing.`,
        h: `For each chunk, the LLM receives the whole document (or a large part) plus the chunk, and is asked for a short context that situates the chunk for search ("This chunk is from ACME's Q2 2025 report, describing interest-rate changes"). The context is prepended to the chunk for embedding and BM25. Prompt caching of the document keeps the cost manageable. Anthropic reported (2024) roughly a third fewer retrieval failures, and more when combined with BM25 and reranking. The original chunk text is still what gets cited; the generated context only helps search.`,
        u: `Use it when contextual headers are not enough — chunks full of references ("the above rate", "this exception") — and when the ingestion budget allows one LLM call per chunk. Measure the gain on your golden set before applying it to everything. Cache document prefixes when the LLM API supports prompt caching, and run the generation as a batch job. Review a sample of generated contexts, because errors there mislead retrieval.`,
        p: `Chunks whose meaning depends on earlier parts of the document are weakly represented by their own text. LLM-written context restores that meaning in the searchable text, making retrieval much more reliable for such documents. Its cost scales with the number of chunks, so for large corpora it is a deliberate investment that should be justified by evaluation results. It is one of the most effective published techniques for improving retrieval on reference-heavy documents.`,
        e: `The chunk "The rate increases by 2% from July" gets the context "From ACME's Q2 2025 report, section on loan interest rates". The question "when do ACME loan rates go up?" now retrieves it, where previously it ranked far down. Generating context for 20,000 chunks with prompt caching took one batch run overnight, and the measured gain on reference-heavy questions justified the cost (illustrative). The context sentences were also shown to reviewers, who found a few wrong ones and corrected the prompt.` },
      { t: `Metadata Inheritance`,
        s: `Every chunk automatically receives its document's metadata — ID, title, version, date, type, access groups.`,
        h: `At chunking time, document-level fields are copied onto each chunk, and chunk-level fields (section path, page range, position) are added. Both are stored as payload in the vector index and as fields in the keyword index, so filters and citations work at chunk level. Chunk-level fields such as page range and section path are added on top of the inherited ones. Inheritance happens in one place in the pipeline, so every chunk gets exactly the same document fields.`,
        u: `Always — filters, security and citations depend on it. When document metadata changes (for example permissions), update all its chunks. Validate that required fields exist on every chunk before indexing; a missing access field should block indexing. Re-propagate fields whenever document metadata changes. It also keeps citations simple, because every chunk already knows its document title, version and page.`,
        p: `A chunk without access groups is either invisible to everyone or visible to everyone; a chunk without a date cannot be filtered by time. Inheritance makes every chunk self-describing. It also keeps indexes consistent: if every chunk carries the same document fields, filters behave the same in the vector store and the keyword engine. Missing or inconsistent fields cause the hardest bugs: answers that are right for some users and silently missing for others.`,
        e: `All 19 chunks of a CV carry doc_id, filename, version and access_groups=["general"], plus their own section path and page. Filtering by access group and citing "CV, page 2, PROJECTS" both work without extra lookups. When the CV's access group changed, all 19 chunks were updated in both indexes within seconds, without re-embedding. Citations for those chunks immediately showed the new version number, because it was inherited from the document.` },
      { t: `Chunk Summaries`,
        s: `A short LLM-written summary of each chunk or section, indexed alongside the original text.`,
        h: `An LLM writes a one- or two-sentence summary per chunk (or per section); the summary is embedded as an extra vector pointing to the chunk, added as a BM25 field, or stored in a separate "summary index" used for broad questions. Answers still quote and cite the original text. Summaries are generated once at ingestion, so they cost nothing at question time. Summaries should describe the content in the words users would use, not repeat headings.`,
        u: `Broad or overview questions, long chunks, tables and code, where the original text does not use the words a user would search with. Generate them with a cheap model in batch; they do not need to be perfect, only descriptive. Review a sample, because a wrong summary can mislead retrieval. They are especially useful for tables, code and slides, whose raw text is a poor match for natural questions.`,
        p: `"What is this document about?" or "which policy covers travel abroad?" match no single detailed chunk well. Summaries describe chunks in plain language, giving broad questions something to match. They also help navigation and interfaces: a list of section summaries is a quick way for users to see what a document covers. They add an LLM call per chunk at ingestion, so use them where broad or vocabulary-mismatched questions are common.`,
        e: `A table chunk's summary "Daily travel allowance per city and employee grade" lets the question "travel allowance rates" find it, even though the table itself contains only numbers and city names. Section summaries also power a 'what does this document cover?' panel in the document viewer. Broad questions such as 'which policies cover travel?' started returning the travel policy's summary chunk first.` },
      { t: `Hypothetical Questions per Chunk`,
        s: `Generating questions that each chunk answers, and indexing those questions too (also called doc2query).`,
        h: `An LLM writes three to five likely user questions per chunk. Each question is embedded (with the query prefix) and points back to the chunk. A user's question now matches question-shaped text instead of document-shaped text, which narrows the vocabulary gap. Questions can be indexed in the same collection with a 'kind' field, or in a separate collection that maps back to chunks. This works for BM25 too, if the questions are indexed as text.`,
        u: `FAQ-style use cases, support knowledge bases, and corpora written in formal language that users do not speak. Generate a few varied questions per chunk, including casual phrasings and abbreviations users really type. Filter out questions the chunk does not actually answer. Real user questions from logs make the best seed examples for the prompt.`,
        p: `Users ask "Can I work from home?" while the policy says "remote working arrangements". Indexing hypothetical questions bridges that mismatch for both dense and keyword search. It costs one LLM call per chunk at ingestion and multiplies the number of vectors, so measure its benefit before applying it to the whole corpus. It is particularly helpful for small embedding models, which struggle most with question-versus-document wording differences.`,
        e: `The remote-work policy chunk gets the questions "Can I work from home?", "How many WFH days are allowed per week?" and "Do I need approval to work remotely?". The user's casual question matches the first one almost exactly. For the HR FAQ, indexing questions raised recall for casual queries, while for technical manuals the gain was small, so it was enabled only for HR (illustrative). The generated questions were also reused as evaluation candidates after review.`,
        c: `
prompt = f"Write 3 short questions a user might ask that this text answers:\\n{chunk.text}"
for q in llm(prompt).splitlines():
    index.add(vector=embed("query: " + q), payload={"chunk_id": chunk.id, "kind": "question"})` },
      { t: `Keyword / Entity Tagging`,
        s: `Extracting key terms and named entities — people, products, places, dates — from each chunk as tags.`,
        h: `Use named-entity recognition (spaCy), keyword extraction (KeyBERT, YAKE) or an LLM to extract tags, normalise them to canonical names, and store them as metadata (filterable fields) and as a boosted BM25 field. Entity normalisation ('ACME Corp' and 'Acme') matters, or one entity becomes several tags. Tags are stored as keyword fields so they can be filtered exactly.`,
        u: `Filters ("product = ZX-200"), entity-centric questions ("everything about supplier X"), faceted search in the interface, and analytics on what the corpus covers. Keep a curated list of important entities (products, teams, systems) to improve extraction accuracy. Show tags as filters in the search interface so users can narrow results themselves. Tags also feed routing: a question mentioning a product can be routed to that product's documents.`,
        p: `Questions about a specific product or person retrieve chunks about similar ones, because embeddings blur names. Tags make exact entity matching and filtering reliable. It also supports analytics: counting chunks per entity shows which products or teams are well documented and which are not. Exact tags also make results explainable: users can see why a document was included by its tags.`,
        e: `Chunks tagged product=ZX-200 let "ZX-200 warranty" filter to the right manual, instead of returning warranty text for the similar ZX-210. A coverage report built from tags showed that one product had almost no documentation, which explained its high abstention rate. Filtering by product tag also halved the candidate set, making reranking faster.` },
      { t: `Orphan Chunk Handling`,
        s: `Dealing with tiny leftover chunks — a lone heading, a single line, a page footer — that carry no meaning on their own.`,
        h: `After splitting, chunks below a minimum size (for example 5–20 tokens) are merged into a neighbour in the same section, usually the following one for headings and the previous one for trailing lines, or dropped if they are boilerplate. Merging must respect section boundaries, so a heading is never attached to a paragraph from another section. Common orphans include page numbers, isolated headings, signature lines and 'continued on next page'.`,
        u: `After any splitting step, especially with heading-based or fixed-size chunking. Set the minimum size in tokens and check its effect on chunk counts per document type. Treat repeated orphans (the same line in many documents) as boilerplate to remove. Review a sample of the smallest chunks after each pipeline change to see what new orphans appear.`,
        p: `Orphans like "Notes:", "Page 4" or "3.4 Exceptions" match random queries and waste context slots, and their citations make no sense to users. They also confuse evaluation, because a retrieved orphan can count as 'relevant' by document while providing no usable evidence. Handling them is cheap and makes every remaining chunk meaningful on its own, which improves both precision and the look of citations.`,
        e: `A 5-token chunk "3.4 Exceptions" is merged into the paragraph that follows it, so the exceptions text keeps its heading and the orphan disappears from results. Merging orphans reduced the chunk count by a few percent and removed a class of meaningless citations such as 'Page 4' from answers. A rule that drops chunks consisting only of page markers removed another class of noise from results.` },
      { t: `Chunk Deduplication`,
        s: `Removing chunks with identical or nearly identical text before indexing.`,
        h: `Exact duplicates are found by hashing normalised text; near-duplicates by MinHash or high embedding similarity. One copy is kept, optionally with links to every source it appeared in, and with the union of their access groups handled carefully. When two copies have different permissions, the stored copy must keep both permission sets so no one loses or gains access. Near-duplicate removal needs a threshold tuned on samples, so genuinely different paragraphs are not merged.`,
        u: `Templates, repeated disclaimers, multiple document versions, overlapping windows and mirrored folders. Run it after chunking and again when assembling context. Track how many chunks were removed per source, which often reveals connector or template problems. Keep a record of removed duplicates so they can be restored if a source turns out to be the authoritative copy.`,
        p: `Duplicates fill the top results with the same text and push out other evidence, and they waste embedding and storage. It also reduces cost: fewer chunks mean less embedding work, smaller indexes and faster search. It also helps evaluation, because duplicated chunks can make retrieval metrics look better or worse than they really are.`,
        e: `The same "Confidentiality notice" chunk in 300 contracts is indexed once, so contract questions retrieve actual clauses instead of five copies of the notice. After deduplication, a question about liability limits retrieved four different relevant clauses instead of the same notice five times. Index size dropped noticeably for the contract collection, and search got slightly faster as a result.` },
      { t: `Chunk IDs & Positions`,
        s: `A stable, deterministic ID for each chunk plus its position — document version, index, page range, character offsets.`,
        h: `The ID is a hash or uuid5 of (document version, chunk index, content hash), so the same input always produces the same ID. Positions allow neighbour expansion (fetch chunks i−1 and i+1), highlighting in a document viewer, exact deletes and lineage. Changing the chunking strategy produces new IDs by design, which is why such changes go into a new index generation. Positions include chunk index, page range and, where available, character offsets in the parsed text.`,
        u: `Every pipeline: idempotent re-processing, citations, debugging and deletes all depend on it. Store positions for every chunk even if no feature uses them yet; neighbour expansion and highlighting are much easier to add later. IDs should be the same in every index (vector and keyword) so results can be fused and deleted consistently.`,
        p: `Random IDs create duplicates on every retry and make deletes and citations unreliable. Deterministic IDs make the whole pipeline safe to re-run. They also link the whole system together: retrieval results, answer citations, feedback and evaluation all refer to chunk IDs. Consistent IDs across stores are what make hybrid search and reconciliation possible at all.`,
        e: `Chunk uuid5(version, 7, sha256) on pages 2–3 can be highlighted in the PDF viewer and fetched together with neighbours 6 and 8 when more context is needed. Feedback on a bad answer points to the exact chunk IDs used, so reviewers can open those chunks and see what the model read. The nightly reconcile job compares chunk IDs between the database and both indexes and repairs any difference.` },
      { t: `Chunking Evaluation`,
        s: `Measuring which chunking strategy actually gives better retrieval and answers, instead of guessing.`,
        h: `Keep the golden questions fixed, rebuild the index with each candidate strategy (size, overlap, method, headers), and compare recall@k, MRR and answer metrics. Label relevance at document or section level, so the labels stay valid when chunk boundaries change. Look at failures per document type, not only averages. Keep latency and index size in the comparison, because a slightly better strategy may cost much more.`,
        u: `Before choosing a strategy, whenever you change parsing or the embedding model, and when new document types are added. Automate it: a script that rebuilds a test index with a given chunking configuration and runs the golden set makes comparisons cheap enough to do often. Include document types and question types in the report, because averages hide where each strategy wins or loses.`,
        p: `Teams copy "chunk size 1,000, overlap 200" from a tutorial and never learn it is wrong for their documents. Evaluation turns chunking from folklore into a measured decision. It also prevents regressions: a change that helps one document type may hurt another, which only per-type evaluation reveals. It also gives a clear story for stakeholders: 'we chose this strategy because it found the right evidence more often on our real questions'.`,
        e: `Illustrative: rebuild the index twice — structure-aware 400-token chunks vs fixed 1,000-token chunks — run the same golden set, and keep the one with the higher recall@5 and answer scores. Repeat when the corpus changes significantly. The comparison script runs in under an hour on a small test index, so chunking experiments became a routine part of pipeline releases. The results are stored with the pipeline version, so the reasoning behind the chosen strategy is documented.` },
    ]},
  ],
});
